1
00:00:00,307 --> 00:00:02,399
Tonight, you'll climb into a soft bed.

2
00:00:02,419 --> 00:00:03,724
A mattress. A pillow.

3
00:00:03,744 --> 00:00:06,331
A warm duvet. You'll close the curtains.

4
00:00:06,351 --> 00:00:08,769
Lock the door. And switch off the light.

5
00:00:08,789 --> 00:00:09,805
And then you'll lie there.

6
00:00:09,825 --> 00:00:11,593
Scrolling. Checking the time.

7
00:00:11,613 --> 00:00:13,654
Wondering why you can't sleep.

8
00:00:13,674 --> 00:00:17,932
You're not alone. In the United States,
about one in three adults doesn't get

9
00:00:17,952 --> 00:00:22,296
enough sleep. We've built the most
comfortable beds in history, and we're still

10
00:00:22,316 --> 00:00:25,901
tired. Now take time back
fifty thousand years.

11
00:00:25,921 --> 00:00:28,347
The sun goes down over the grassland.

12
00:00:28,367 --> 00:00:30,867
A hunter lies down on the bare earth.

13
00:00:30,887 --> 00:00:32,192
No mattress. No pillow.

14
00:00:32,212 --> 00:00:33,794
No walls. No door.

15
00:00:33,814 --> 00:00:37,964
And somewhere out there in the
dark, the lions are just waking up.

16
00:00:37,984 --> 00:00:40,689
Now your brain is
probably saying, Cavemen?

17
00:00:40,709 --> 00:00:42,139
They slept on rocks.

18
00:00:42,159 --> 00:00:43,951
And they barely slept at all.

19
00:00:43,971 --> 00:00:45,201
Cold, hard ground.

20
00:00:45,221 --> 00:00:47,003
One eye open, all night long.

21
00:00:47,023 --> 00:00:48,680
At least, that's what we assume.

22
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:50,802
But scientists have found their beds.

23
00:00:50,822 --> 00:00:53,731
Real beds, about two
hundred thousand years old.

24
00:00:53,751 --> 00:00:57,893
They've found leaves that may have
been chosen to fight insects.

25
00:00:57,913 --> 00:01:01,856
They've watched apes build a
fresh bed every single night.

26
00:01:01,876 --> 00:01:05,981
And they've put sleep trackers on
hunter-gatherers, and found something nobody

27
00:01:06,001 --> 00:01:11,557
expected. To understand it, we first
have to see why sleep is so dangerous.

28
00:01:11,577 --> 00:01:13,302
Think about what sleep really is.

29
00:01:13,322 --> 00:01:14,899
For hours, you can't see.

30
00:01:14,919 --> 00:01:17,652
You can't run. You barely
hear what's coming.

31
00:01:17,672 --> 00:01:20,998
For a wild animal, that
sounds like a terrible idea.

32
00:01:21,018 --> 00:01:25,936
And yet, almost every animal scientists
have studied sleeps, or does something

33
00:01:25,956 --> 00:01:30,829
very like it. Some birds and dolphins
even sleep with half of their brain at a

34
00:01:30,849 --> 00:01:35,159
time. One half rests, while
the other half keeps watch.

35
00:01:35,179 --> 00:01:36,903
And you do a little of this too.

36
00:01:36,923 --> 00:01:41,329
In twenty sixteen, scientists studied
people sleeping in a new place for the

37
00:01:41,349 --> 00:01:46,366
first time. During deep sleep, one half
of the brain stayed more alert than the

38
00:01:46,386 --> 00:01:49,749
other. It reacted more to strange sounds.

39
00:01:49,769 --> 00:01:54,659
That may be one reason you sleep so
badly on your first night in a hotel.

40
00:01:54,679 --> 00:01:56,567
So sleep must be worth the risk.

41
00:01:56,587 --> 00:02:01,879
In nineteen sixty four, a seventeen year
old student named Randy Gardner tried to

42
00:02:01,899 --> 00:02:04,229
stay awake as long as he could.

43
00:02:04,249 --> 00:02:06,701
He lasted about eleven days.

44
00:02:06,721 --> 00:02:11,376
By the end, he struggled to concentrate,
lost track of what he was saying, and

45
00:02:11,396 --> 00:02:13,194
saw things that weren't there.

46
00:02:13,214 --> 00:02:16,423
At one point, he mistook a
street sign for a person.

47
00:02:16,443 --> 00:02:18,168
Afterwards, he recovered.

48
00:02:18,188 --> 00:02:22,248
Without enough of it, memory, mood
and reactions all start to fail.

49
00:02:22,268 --> 00:02:24,597
Sleep also does some of
the brain's cleaning.

50
00:02:24,617 --> 00:02:29,395
In twenty thirteen, scientists
watched the brains of sleeping mice.

51
00:02:29,415 --> 00:02:34,183
The gaps between their brain cells opened
up, and waste washed out faster than

52
00:02:34,203 --> 00:02:35,658
when they were awake.

53
00:02:35,678 --> 00:02:39,040
And for our ancestors, the
night was full of hunters.

54
00:02:39,060 --> 00:02:41,628
Lions do much of their hunting after dark.

55
00:02:41,648 --> 00:02:44,032
So do hyenas. And leopards.

56
00:02:44,052 --> 00:02:45,761
And these weren't idle threats.

57
00:02:45,781 --> 00:02:50,975
In a South African cave called Swartkrans,
scientists found part of a skull, from

58
00:02:50,995 --> 00:02:53,059
a young relative of ours.

59
00:02:53,079 --> 00:02:54,740
It had two neat holes in it.

60
00:02:54,760 --> 00:02:58,465
The researcher Bob Brain
noticed how far apart they were.

61
00:02:58,485 --> 00:03:01,194
They matched the lower fangs of a leopard.

62
00:03:01,214 --> 00:03:05,982
He suggested that leopards dragged their
prey up into trees near the cave, and

63
00:03:06,002 --> 00:03:07,671
the bones fell in.

64
00:03:07,691 --> 00:03:10,175
He called his book, The
Hunters or the Hunted?

65
00:03:10,195 --> 00:03:13,043
For a very long time,
our relatives were both.

66
00:03:13,063 --> 00:03:16,133
So where do you sleep, when
the night is full of teeth?

67
00:03:16,153 --> 00:03:18,292
Look at our closest living relatives.

68
00:03:18,312 --> 00:03:23,110
Every evening, a chimpanzee climbs
into a tree, and builds itself a bed.

69
00:03:23,130 --> 00:03:26,247
It bends branches into a springy platform.

70
00:03:26,267 --> 00:03:29,686
Then it lines it with leafy
twigs, like a mattress.

71
00:03:29,706 --> 00:03:31,554
And chimps can be picky about it.

72
00:03:31,574 --> 00:03:37,180
In Uganda, scientists found chimps choosing
one kind of tree far more often than

73
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:41,730
any other. It made up less than a tenth
of the trees, but held nearly three

74
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:43,492
quarters of the nests.

75
00:03:43,512 --> 00:03:45,978
Its branches are stiff and strong.

76
00:03:45,998 --> 00:03:49,591
They make a firm, steady bed
that won't sag in the night.

77
00:03:49,611 --> 00:03:51,848
The whole job takes just a few minutes.

78
00:03:51,868 --> 00:03:54,585
And most nights, it
builds a brand new one.

79
00:03:54,605 --> 00:03:57,710
Gorillas and orangutans build beds too.

80
00:03:57,730 --> 00:04:02,176
When it rains, orangutans
sometimes add a roof of leaves.

81
00:04:02,196 --> 00:04:07,869
In twenty eighteen, scientists swabbed
forty one chimpanzee nests in Tanzania,

82
00:04:07,889 --> 00:04:10,160
and looked at the bacteria inside.

83
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:14,898
Earlier research on homes in the United
States had found that about a third of

84
00:04:14,918 --> 00:04:18,548
the bacteria in our beds
come from our own bodies.

85
00:04:18,568 --> 00:04:22,210
Skin. Mouths. And, well, the other end.

86
00:04:22,230 --> 00:04:25,903
In the chimp nests, it was only
about three and a half percent.

87
00:04:25,923 --> 00:04:30,732
And in all the nests they checked for
bugs, they found just four parasites.

88
00:04:30,752 --> 00:04:34,509
In that way, at least, the chimps'
beds were cleaner than ours.

89
00:04:34,529 --> 00:04:38,263
Think about it. You might change
your sheets every week or two.

90
00:04:38,283 --> 00:04:41,332
A chimp gets a brand new bed every night.

91
00:04:41,352 --> 00:04:46,295
Many scientists think our early ancestors
once slept the same way, high up in the

92
00:04:46,315 --> 00:04:49,644
trees. Up there, lions and
hyenas can't reach you.

93
00:04:49,664 --> 00:04:51,297
But then, something changed.

94
00:04:51,317 --> 00:04:54,685
About two million years ago,
a new kind of human appeared.

95
00:04:54,705 --> 00:04:56,523
Homo erectus. Tall.

96
00:04:56,543 --> 00:04:59,382
Long legs. Built for
walking, not for climbing.

97
00:04:59,402 --> 00:05:03,969
Some researchers think that by around
this time, our ancestors had left their

98
00:05:03,989 --> 00:05:07,253
tree nests, and started
sleeping on the ground.

99
00:05:07,273 --> 00:05:09,403
So how did they survive the night?

100
00:05:09,423 --> 00:05:12,356
Some researchers think fire
was part of the answer.

101
00:05:12,376 --> 00:05:14,556
Fire gives light. It gives warmth.

102
00:05:14,576 --> 00:05:17,380
And most animals keep
their distance from it.

103
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:20,195
It may be what made sleeping
on the ground possible.

104
00:05:20,215 --> 00:05:25,032
Some of the oldest clear evidence of fire
comes from Wonderwerk Cave, in South

105
00:05:25,052 --> 00:05:27,884
Africa, about a million years ago.

106
00:05:27,904 --> 00:05:31,408
And remember Swartkrans, the
cave with the leopard bite?

107
00:05:31,428 --> 00:05:35,446
In its younger layers, Bob
Brain found burned bones.

108
00:05:35,466 --> 00:05:38,961
Possibly some of the oldest
signs of fire anywhere.

109
00:05:38,981 --> 00:05:43,424
Brain thought that with fire, the
balance of power in the cave shifted.

110
00:05:43,444 --> 00:05:45,848
The hunted were becoming the hunters.

111
00:05:45,868 --> 00:05:49,248
A later re-test found the
evidence much less clear.

112
00:05:49,268 --> 00:05:51,461
But it's a powerful idea.

113
00:05:51,481 --> 00:05:53,001
And something else changed too.

114
00:05:53,021 --> 00:05:57,358
Our sleep itself. Compared with other
primates, humans sleep the least.

115
00:05:57,378 --> 00:06:02,295
Based on our body and brain size, scientists
expected us to sleep around nine and

116
00:06:02,315 --> 00:06:05,265
a half hours. We sleep about seven.

117
00:06:05,285 --> 00:06:08,105
But we spend more of our sleep in REM.

118
00:06:08,125 --> 00:06:11,068
That's the stage where
most vivid dreams happen.

119
00:06:11,088 --> 00:06:13,885
Our sleep seems to have
got shorter, but deeper.

120
00:06:13,905 --> 00:06:18,472
One idea is that sleeping by
the fire, in a group, was safer.

121
00:06:18,492 --> 00:06:23,254
So we could get away with less of it, and
spend the extra hours awake, together.

122
00:06:23,274 --> 00:06:24,071
Talking. Teaching.

123
00:06:24,091 --> 00:06:26,730
Making tools. And we didn't sleep alone.

124
00:06:26,750 --> 00:06:28,143
We slept in groups.

125
00:06:28,163 --> 00:06:30,509
Close together, around the fire.

126
00:06:30,529 --> 00:06:33,235
So what did these early
beds actually look like?

127
00:06:33,255 --> 00:06:38,243
For that, we have to go to a cliff on
the border of South Africa and Eswatini.

128
00:06:38,263 --> 00:06:40,521
There's a cave there called Border Cave.

129
00:06:40,541 --> 00:06:44,742
People used it, on and off, for more
than two hundred thousand years.

130
00:06:44,762 --> 00:06:50,251
In twenty twenty, a team led by the
archaeologist Lyn Wadley dug into its oldest

131
00:06:50,271 --> 00:06:52,791
layers. And they found grass.

132
00:06:52,811 --> 00:06:57,395
Thin layers of it, laid down on
purpose, on top of a layer of ash.

133
00:06:57,415 --> 00:07:00,158
Bedding, roughly two
hundred thousand years old.

134
00:07:00,178 --> 00:07:01,746
Why put grass on ash?

135
00:07:01,766 --> 00:07:06,259
The researchers think the ash may have
helped keep crawling insects away.

136
00:07:06,279 --> 00:07:10,225
Ants can't easily walk
through fine, powdery ash.

137
00:07:10,245 --> 00:07:13,537
Some of the bedding also
held bits of camphor bush.

138
00:07:13,557 --> 00:07:17,803
A strong-smelling plant that's
still used to keep insects away.

139
00:07:17,823 --> 00:07:20,178
They didn't just lie down on the floor.

140
00:07:20,198 --> 00:07:24,190
They built a bed, and it looks
like they built it against bugs.

141
00:07:24,210 --> 00:07:25,965
And they didn't only sleep on it.

142
00:07:25,985 --> 00:07:30,577
The bedding is full of tiny grains of
red ochre, and chips of stone from

143
00:07:30,597 --> 00:07:35,229
tool-making. It was a bed,
a sofa, and a workbench.

144
00:07:35,249 --> 00:07:40,054
Now jump forward to about seventy seven
thousand years ago, to a rock shelter

145
00:07:40,074 --> 00:07:44,731
called Sibudu. Here, people made mats of
sedges and rushes, and they topped them

146
00:07:44,751 --> 00:07:48,884
with leaves. The leaves came from
a tree called Cryptocarya woodii.

147
00:07:48,904 --> 00:07:52,413
Its leaves contain chemicals
that can kill mosquitoes.

148
00:07:52,433 --> 00:07:54,822
And mosquitoes carry disease.

149
00:07:54,842 --> 00:07:56,122
Was it on purpose?

150
00:07:56,142 --> 00:07:57,447
We can't be certain.

151
00:07:57,467 --> 00:08:02,297
But they kept making beds like this, layer
after layer, for nearly forty thousand

152
00:08:02,317 --> 00:08:07,540
years. Some of the leaves are so well
preserved, you can still see their veins.

153
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:12,363
And there's more. Then, from about seventy
three thousand years ago, they started

154
00:08:12,383 --> 00:08:14,075
burning their old bedding.

155
00:08:14,095 --> 00:08:17,719
Probably to clear out pests
and rubbish, and start fresh.

156
00:08:17,739 --> 00:08:20,580
A clean bed. No washing machine required.

157
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:22,288
And these weren't the only ones.

158
00:08:22,308 --> 00:08:26,925
About twenty three thousand years ago, on
the shore of the Sea of Galilee, people

159
00:08:26,945 --> 00:08:28,965
built huts out of branches.

160
00:08:28,985 --> 00:08:33,189
The site lay underwater until the
lake dropped in nineteen eighty nine.

161
00:08:33,209 --> 00:08:38,614
Inside one hut, archaeologists found
bundles of grass, laid along the walls,

162
00:08:38,634 --> 00:08:40,497
around a fire in the middle.

163
00:08:40,517 --> 00:08:43,292
Grass to lie on. A fire for warmth.

164
00:08:43,312 --> 00:08:44,542
Walls of branches.

165
00:08:44,562 --> 00:08:46,766
Not so different from a bedroom.

166
00:08:46,786 --> 00:08:48,809
But a bed is only half the story.

167
00:08:48,829 --> 00:08:51,050
The other half is sleep itself.

168
00:08:51,070 --> 00:08:55,575
We can't measure the sleep of someone
who died fifty thousand years ago.

169
00:08:55,595 --> 00:08:58,306
But we can measure the next best thing.

170
00:08:58,326 --> 00:09:03,585
In twenty fifteen, the sleep scientists
Gandhi Yetish and Jerome Siegel studied

171
00:09:03,605 --> 00:09:07,122
three groups of people who
still live without electricity.

172
00:09:07,142 --> 00:09:08,786
The Hadza in Tanzania.

173
00:09:08,806 --> 00:09:10,186
The San in Namibia.

174
00:09:10,206 --> 00:09:12,332
And the Tsimane in Bolivia.

175
00:09:12,352 --> 00:09:16,249
They wore sleep trackers on their
wrists, hundreds of nights in all.

176
00:09:16,269 --> 00:09:18,999
Most people expected them to sleep a lot.

177
00:09:19,019 --> 00:09:22,228
Nine hours. Ten. That's
not what they found.

178
00:09:22,248 --> 00:09:27,441
They slept between about five point
seven and seven point one hours a night.

179
00:09:27,461 --> 00:09:29,702
About the same as many of us.

180
00:09:29,722 --> 00:09:34,140
And in winter, when nights are longer,
two of the groups slept nearly an hour

181
00:09:34,160 --> 00:09:38,175
more. They didn't go to bed
when the sun went down, either.

182
00:09:38,195 --> 00:09:42,738
On average, they fell asleep more
than three hours after sunset.

183
00:09:42,758 --> 00:09:45,461
And they usually woke before sunrise.

184
00:09:45,481 --> 00:09:47,173
So what decided when they slept?

185
00:09:47,193 --> 00:09:51,437
Temperature. Their sleep lined up
with the coolest part of the night.

186
00:09:51,457 --> 00:09:56,137
They fell asleep as the air cooled, and
woke near the coldest point, just before

187
00:09:56,157 --> 00:09:59,298
dawn. And here's the part
that should make you jealous.

188
00:09:59,318 --> 00:10:01,048
Insomnia was rare.

189
00:10:01,068 --> 00:10:04,701
So rare that two of the groups
don't even have a word for it.

190
00:10:04,721 --> 00:10:06,826
They slept about as long as us.

191
00:10:06,846 --> 00:10:09,747
But without lying awake
staring at the ceiling.

192
00:10:09,767 --> 00:10:11,273
So what's different?

193
00:10:11,293 --> 00:10:12,641
One answer is light.

194
00:10:12,661 --> 00:10:14,741
Your body has an internal clock.

195
00:10:14,761 --> 00:10:16,912
And the clock is set by light.

196
00:10:16,932 --> 00:10:20,562
Inside your eyes, there are
special light-sensing cells.

197
00:10:20,582 --> 00:10:22,637
Their main job isn't seeing.

198
00:10:22,657 --> 00:10:25,512
It's telling your brain
whether it's day or night.

199
00:10:25,532 --> 00:10:28,065
And they're most sensitive to blue light.

200
00:10:28,085 --> 00:10:32,465
In twenty thirteen, the scientist Kenneth
Wright took a group of people camping

201
00:10:32,485 --> 00:10:33,978
in the Rocky Mountains.

202
00:10:33,998 --> 00:10:35,908
For a week. No phones.

203
00:10:35,928 --> 00:10:38,483
No torches. No electric light at all.

204
00:10:38,503 --> 00:10:40,616
Just the sun, and a campfire.

205
00:10:40,636 --> 00:10:44,866
Before the trip, he measured a
sleep hormone called melatonin.

206
00:10:44,886 --> 00:10:48,968
It rises in the evening, and
tells your body it's time for bed.

207
00:10:48,988 --> 00:10:53,418
After a week outdoors, it started
rising about two hours earlier.

208
00:10:53,438 --> 00:10:56,320
Night owls turned into early birds.

209
00:10:56,340 --> 00:10:58,961
Their body clocks had
moved, to match the sun.

210
00:10:58,981 --> 00:11:04,586
A later study found that even a weekend
of camping moved people's clocks earlier.

211
00:11:04,606 --> 00:11:09,321
And a week of winter camping moved them
by more than two and a half hours.

212
00:11:09,341 --> 00:11:14,258
The researchers also measured the light
at night in those hunter-gatherer camps.

213
00:11:14,278 --> 00:11:18,593
Even with campfires burning,
it stayed incredibly dim.

214
00:11:18,613 --> 00:11:23,643
For almost all of human history, nights
were lit by fire, or not at all.

215
00:11:23,663 --> 00:11:28,394
Electric light only reached most
homes in the last century or so.

216
00:11:28,414 --> 00:11:29,970
Now think about your evenings.

217
00:11:29,990 --> 00:11:31,250
Bright ceiling lights.

218
00:11:31,270 --> 00:11:33,962
Laptops. Phones, inches from your face.

219
00:11:33,982 --> 00:11:37,400
All of that tells your brain
the day isn't over yet.

220
00:11:37,420 --> 00:11:42,564
So your clock gets pushed later, while
your alarm stays exactly where it is.

221
00:11:42,584 --> 00:11:44,765
Our ancestors had one light at night.

222
00:11:44,785 --> 00:11:47,056
A fire. Dim, and orange.

223
00:11:47,076 --> 00:11:52,037
When people settled down and built houses,
beds started to rise off the ground.

224
00:11:52,057 --> 00:11:56,762
About five thousand years ago, on the
Orkney Islands, north of Scotland, there

225
00:11:56,782 --> 00:11:59,153
was a village called Skara Brae.

226
00:11:59,173 --> 00:12:04,278
The village lay buried in sand until a
great storm in eighteen fifty uncovered

227
00:12:04,298 --> 00:12:07,635
it. Its houses still have
their furniture, made of stone.

228
00:12:07,655 --> 00:12:09,710
Stone dressers. Stone hearths.

229
00:12:09,730 --> 00:12:12,991
And stone box beds, on
either side of the fire.

230
00:12:13,011 --> 00:12:15,604
Nobody knows exactly what filled them.

231
00:12:15,624 --> 00:12:18,602
Perhaps heather, bracken or animal skins.

232
00:12:18,622 --> 00:12:23,332
In ancient Egypt, many people rested
their heads on something surprising.

233
00:12:23,352 --> 00:12:27,542
Not a pillow. A headrest,
carved from wood or stone.

234
00:12:27,562 --> 00:12:31,555
The young pharaoh Tutankhamun
was buried with eight of them.

235
00:12:31,575 --> 00:12:35,741
One is carved from ivory,
held up by a kneeling god.

236
00:12:35,761 --> 00:12:39,516
In the Americas, people slept off
the ground in a different way.

237
00:12:39,536 --> 00:12:41,513
Hanging between two trees.

238
00:12:41,533 --> 00:12:46,213
The first European to write about one
was Christopher Columbus, in fourteen

239
00:12:46,233 --> 00:12:51,920
ninety two. The word hammock comes from
the Taino language of the Caribbean.

240
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:57,020
And the word mattress traces back to an
Arabic word for a cushion, or a place

241
00:12:57,040 --> 00:12:59,310
where something is thrown down.

242
00:12:59,330 --> 00:13:02,363
Then there's a strange idea
about sleep in old Europe.

243
00:13:02,383 --> 00:13:07,726
The historian Roger Ekirch found hundreds
of old references to a first sleep, and

244
00:13:07,746 --> 00:13:12,528
a second sleep. People went to bed soon
after dark, woke up in the middle of the

245
00:13:12,548 --> 00:13:16,476
night for an hour or so,
and then went back to sleep.

246
00:13:16,496 --> 00:13:19,725
Some people say this is how
humans are meant to sleep.

247
00:13:19,745 --> 00:13:23,882
But in the three societies Yetish
studied, there was no sign of it.

248
00:13:23,902 --> 00:13:28,820
One farming village in Madagascar did
show a middle-of-the-night wake-up, on

249
00:13:28,840 --> 00:13:30,582
about half of all nights.

250
00:13:30,602 --> 00:13:35,732
So it may be a habit that comes with
long, dark nights, rather than the way all

251
00:13:35,752 --> 00:13:37,894
humans have always slept.

252
00:13:37,914 --> 00:13:40,944
Remember the hunter-gatherers
with the sleep trackers?

253
00:13:40,964 --> 00:13:42,369
There's one more study.

254
00:13:42,389 --> 00:13:44,497
And it's the strangest of all.

255
00:13:44,517 --> 00:13:49,571
In twenty seventeen, the researcher David
Samson tracked a Hadza camp for twenty

256
00:13:49,591 --> 00:13:54,709
days. He looked at every minute of every
night, and asked one simple question.

257
00:13:54,729 --> 00:13:57,614
How often is everyone
asleep at the same time?

258
00:13:57,634 --> 00:13:59,450
The answer? Almost never.

259
00:13:59,470 --> 00:14:04,194
In more than two hundred and twenty hours
of night, everyone was asleep at once

260
00:14:04,214 --> 00:14:06,354
for just eighteen minutes.

261
00:14:06,374 --> 00:14:08,829
The rest of the time, someone was awake.

262
00:14:08,849 --> 00:14:11,216
On average, more than
a third of the group.

263
00:14:11,236 --> 00:14:12,966
Poking the fire. Listening.

264
00:14:12,986 --> 00:14:15,483
Watching. And it wasn't random.

265
00:14:15,503 --> 00:14:19,383
Older people tended to go to
bed early, and wake up early.

266
00:14:19,403 --> 00:14:21,498
Younger people stayed up later.

267
00:14:21,518 --> 00:14:22,918
Like a night watch.

268
00:14:22,938 --> 00:14:24,522
Except nobody planned it.

269
00:14:24,542 --> 00:14:26,372
Now think about your grandparents.

270
00:14:26,392 --> 00:14:27,909
Up at five in the morning.

271
00:14:27,929 --> 00:14:30,167
Asleep in their chair by nine.

272
00:14:30,187 --> 00:14:32,474
We usually think of that as a problem.

273
00:14:32,494 --> 00:14:35,149
The researchers have a name for this idea.

274
00:14:35,169 --> 00:14:38,350
The poorly sleeping
grandparent hypothesis.

275
00:14:38,370 --> 00:14:42,896
Your grandparents' early mornings might
be a leftover from a time when somebody

276
00:14:42,916 --> 00:14:44,994
always had to be awake.

277
00:14:45,014 --> 00:14:49,544
And babies? In many cultures, babies
sleep within reach of their mothers.

278
00:14:49,564 --> 00:14:52,620
It's almost universal
among other primates.

279
00:14:52,640 --> 00:14:57,883
Putting a baby to sleep alone, in a room
of its own, is a fairly recent habit.

280
00:14:57,903 --> 00:15:02,880
For most of human history, sleeping alone
would have been the dangerous thing.

281
00:15:02,900 --> 00:15:06,667
So did ancient humans sleep on
bare rocks, with one eye open?

282
00:15:06,687 --> 00:15:10,476
Not quite. They slept on
beds that fought bugs.

283
00:15:10,496 --> 00:15:13,263
Around a fire that kept the night away.

284
00:15:13,283 --> 00:15:17,463
And they could sleep deeply, because
someone else was keeping one eye open for

285
00:15:17,483 --> 00:15:18,518
them.

286
00:15:18,538 --> 00:15:20,917
Today, we sleep behind locked doors.

287
00:15:20,937 --> 00:15:24,844
On memory foam. With blackout
curtains, and white noise.

288
00:15:24,864 --> 00:15:27,648
We're safer at night than
any humans in history.

289
00:15:27,668 --> 00:15:30,198
And yet, millions of us lie awake.

290
00:15:30,218 --> 00:15:34,117
Staring at a glowing screen,
long after the sun has gone.

291
00:15:34,137 --> 00:15:38,261
Fifty thousand years ago, a hunter
lay down on a bed of grass.

292
00:15:38,281 --> 00:15:40,898
He had leaves to keep the insects away.

293
00:15:40,918 --> 00:15:43,211
A fire to keep the lions back.

294
00:15:43,231 --> 00:15:47,730
And somewhere in the dark, an old
woman, sitting up, wide awake.

295
00:15:47,750 --> 00:15:49,133
Your bed has a mattress.

296
00:15:49,153 --> 00:15:50,991
Theirs had a guard.
